        <div id="rain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Can You Run a Generator in the Rain With a Cover? Yes, Outdoors Only
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, with a proper running cover and the generator in its normal outdoor spot. Generator manuals warn against operating in rain or snow without protection because water on the outlets and control panel creates a shock hazard. A running tent is the purpose-made answer: it keeps water off the panel while leaving the sides open for airflow. Our full guide to <a href="/can-you-run-a-generator-in-the-rain/">running a generator in the rain</a> covers the electrical side in more depth. Touch plugs and switches with dry hands only.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Running Cover Protects</h3>

          <p>
            The canopy shields the outlet panel, the plug ends of your cords, the air filter intake and the fuel cap from direct rain and falling snow. Keep cord connections off the ground and under the canopy edge too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Does Not Protect Against</h3>

          <p>
            A running cover does nothing about standing water. Never run a generator in a puddle, on flooded ground or where water can pool around the base, cover or no cover. Set it on a raised, level, firm surface such as pavers or a small platform. A cover also does nothing about carbon monoxide, which the next section deals with, and it does not make it safe to tuck the generator under an overhang to get it out of the rain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Carbon Monoxide: A Cover Is Not a Shelter
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the most important section on the page. Portable generator exhaust contains carbon monoxide, a gas you cannot see or smell, and generator CO poisoning kills people in the US every year, often during the storms that make people want to protect the generator from the weather. A running cover keeps rain off a generator that is already in a safe outdoor location. It does not make any other location safe.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Indoors, in a Garage or Under a Carport</h3>

          <p>
            Do not run a generator inside a house, basement, garage or shed, even with the door open, and do not run it under a carport, porch roof or covered patio. Champion&#39;s Storm Shield page says plainly not to run the generator in a garage or under a porch. A tent around the generator does not change any of that: CO still drifts to wherever the air goes, which in a partly enclosed space is toward the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">20 Feet Away, Exhaust Pointed Away</h3>

          <p>
            The CDC and the Consumer Product Safety Commission advise placing a portable generator at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust directed away from windows, doors and vents, and generator manuals carry the same warning. Further is better. Our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> walks through choosing that spot,.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Put CO Alarms in the House</h3>

          <p>
            Install battery-powered or battery-backup CO alarms on every level of the home and outside sleeping areas before outage season, and test them. A generator with a built-in CO shutoff protects against a build-up at the machine, not against exhaust drifting through a window. Our <a href="/best-carbon-monoxide-detector/">best carbon monoxide detectors</a> guide covers which alarms keep working when the power is out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Materials and NFPA Flame Ratings: What the Listings Say
          </h2>

          <p>
            A running cover sits a foot or two above a hot engine and muffler, so the fabric matters more than it does on a storage cover. The listings for the covers on this page use a few different materials, and only some of them cite a flame rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ripstop Vinyl and NFPA 701</h3>

          <p>
            GenTent describes its canopies as waterproof ripstop vinyl rated to NFPA 701, the National Fire Protection Association standard for flame propagation in textiles, and some retailer listings add that components meet UL 94 V-0 for plastics. NFPA 701 means the fabric meets a standard that limits how flame spreads; it does not mean the fabric is fireproof or that it can touch a muffler. GenTent lists a working temperature range of roughly minus 15 to 185 degrees Fahrenheit for the canopy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wind-snow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Wind and Snow Ratings: How Much Weather a Tent Is Built For
          </h2>

          <p>
            Storm outages come with weather, and a tent that blows off or caves in under wet snow is no use at 2 a.m. GenTent-built tents publish specific ratings; budget covers usually do not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wind</h3>

          <p>
            Champion lists the Storm Shield at up to 70 mph winds and cites independent severe-weather ratings for it. Westinghouse lists the same 70 mph figure for the WGenTent, and GenTent listings cite 70 mph forecast winds for the standard kits. Because these tents clamp to the frame, the generator&#39;s own weight anchors the canopy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Snow Load</h3>

          <p>
            The same listings cite an 18-inch snow load, which GenTent equates to about 55 pounds, and Champion lists up to 18 inches of snow per day. Brush heavy snow off the canopy during long storms anyway: wet snow is much heavier than dry, and a sagging canopy sits closer to the hot engine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Setting Up a Generator Tent Before the Storm
          </h2>

          <p>
            The worst time to set up a tent for the first time is in the dark, in the rain, with the power already out. Fit it in daylight when you first buy it, and leave it on the generator or stored beside it so it is ready.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Clamp It</h3>

          <p>
            GenTent and Champion describe a three-step setup: clamp, frame, cover. The clamps or frame adapters grip the generator&#39;s tube frame (or, on inverter kits, the case or handle). Position them so nothing blocks the exhaust outlet, the air intake or the outlet panel, and check that the clamps are tight before the wind picks up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Frame It</h3>

          <p>
            Rods or a rigid frame slot into the clamps and hold the canopy above the generator. Make sure the canopy rides well above the muffler. If the frame sits crooked, the canopy can sag toward the hot side of the engine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Cover It</h3>

          <p>
            Pull the canopy over the frame, secure it, and leave the sides open as designed. Route cords out under the canopy edge so the plug ends stay dry, and use outdoor-rated cords sized for the load and the distance back to the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Recheck the Spot</h3>

          <p>
            With the tent on, confirm the generator is still 20 feet or more from the house, the exhaust points away from every window, door and vent, and the base is out of standing water. Then start it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Storing a Generator and Its Cover Between Outages
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most of a generator&#39;s life is spent waiting, and how you store it decides whether it starts at the next outage. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-store-a-portable-generator/">how to store a portable generator</a> covers fuel and battery care. Storing a cold generator in a garage or shed is fine; running it there never is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cool First, Then Cover</h3>

          <p>
            After running, let the generator cool completely before fitting a storage cover. The muffler and engine stay hot well after shutdown, and a fabric cover pressed against them can scorch or ignite. Store the generator with fuel handled as the manual directs, away from flames and pilot lights.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leave the Tent On or Take It Off?</h3>

          <p>
            A running tent can stay on a generator that lives outdoors on a pad, which also keeps rain off the controls between outages. Dry a wet tent before folding it away to avoid mildew.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Generator Under a Storage Cover</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A storage cover traps heat around the engine and muffler and can block the exhaust and cooling vents. It is a fire risk the moment the engine starts. Storage covers go on cold, switched-off generators only. If you need weather protection while it runs, buy a running tent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a Tent as Permission to Run It in the Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A running cover keeps rain off a generator that is already outdoors, 20 feet from the house with the exhaust pointed away. It does not make a garage, carport, porch or shed safe. Carbon monoxide builds up in any partly enclosed space and drifts indoors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by Wattage Instead of Frame Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wattage is a rough filter. Two generators of the same wattage can have very different frames, and inverters need different tents entirely. Measure the frame perimeter without wheels and handles, or use the maker&#39;s fit tool, before ordering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Covering a Hot Generator Right After Shutdown</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The muffler stays hot long after the engine stops. Throwing a storage cover over it straight away can scorch the fabric or start a fire. Let the generator cool fully, then cover it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Standing Water Under the Tent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tent keeps rain off the top, not water off the ground. Running a generator in a puddle or on flooded ground is a shock and damage risk. Set it on a raised, level pad before the storm arrives.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

